ssioned which said that 30  hours on duolingo was equivalent to one  year language study at a university now  how can they possibly make a statement  like that which language which  university which learner which learner  on duolingo which learner at a  university and you know and the test was  the course of multiple choice so yeah  you can get stuff right on a multiple  choice but does that mean that in the  future when you go to use these words or  use this grammatical pattern that you're  going to use correctly not necessarily  and so 30 hours on duolingo in terms of  exposure to the language is is really  nothing they tell you 5 hour 5 minutes a  day 5 minutes a day how long does it  take to get 30 hours lots you know even  if you did an hour a day that's 30 days  so you know whatever 12 12 times 12 a  year a year you get to 30 hours I'm  doing the math in my head here but um  that's nothing 30 hours is nothing if I  look at what I've done in Greek I've  listened to gosh I think I've listened  an hour a day over the last three months  I've listened 100 hours and I've spent I  don't know how many maybe another 50  hours reading and saving and reviewing  stuff like that and still it's only  gradually coming to me and sometimes  it's there and sometimes I don't have it  and so you don't master I've often said  this language learning is not a matter  of you know building blocks or bricks  that you build this solid thing it's  more a matter of of immersing yourself  and letting the brain develop some new  habits that are going to be superimposed  on habits that you already have habits  that you have for other languages so I'm  saying this because I just want to  debunk the idea  we can master the basics you know and  that you know you learn in 30 hours on  duolingo you learn more than a  university course all you're doing is  getting some exposure to the language  and I highly doubt that the exposure on  duolingo and a very artificial  environment where you're simply